fr:mainmatter.html:p:
    Let A and B be types. The coproduct (disjoint sum) A+B is a typed defined by the following constructors:

\text {inl}:A\to  A+B

\text {inr}:B\to  A+B
    For any type family indexed by x:A+B, satisfies the following induction principle:

        \text {ind}_+: (\Pi _{(x:A)}P(\text {inl}(x)))\to (\Pi _{(y:B)}P(\text {inr}(y)))\to \Pi _{(z:A+B)}P(z)

    (Note that sometimes we denoted \text {ind}_+(f,g) as [f,g]) And the computation rule:

        \begin {align*}
            \text {ind}_+(f,g,\text {inl}(x))&\equiv  f(x)\\
            \text {ind}_+(f,g,\text {inr}(y))&\equiv  g(y)
        \end {align*}

    where f and g are defined:

        \begin {align*}
            f&:\Pi _{(x:A)}P(\text {inl}(x))\\
            g&:\Pi _{(y:B)}P(\text {inr}(y))
        \end {align*}

    This can be presented by pattern matching to define a function h:\Pi  _{(z:A+B)}.P(z):

        \begin {align*}
            h(\text {inl}(x))&:\equiv  f(x)\\
            h(\text {inr}(y))&:\equiv  g(y)
        \end {align*}

The elimination rule is formed with two projections.

    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  p:(x:A)\times  B(x)
    }{
        \begin {align*}
            \Gamma &\vdash  \text {pr}_A(p):A\\
            \Gamma &\vdash  \text {pr}_B(p):B(\text {pr}_A(p))
        \end {align*}
    }

Consider a type A in context \Gamma . A family of types over A in context \Gamma is a type B(x) in context \Gamma , x:A.
\Gamma , x:A \vdash B(x) \space \text {type}
B is a family of types over A in context \Gamma . Alternatively, we say that B(x) is a type indexed by x:A in context \Gamma .

html:li.fr:tex:
\frac {
    \Gamma \vdash  A\equiv  B\space \text {type}
}{
    \Gamma \vdash  A\space \text {type}
}
\quad 
\frac {
    \Gamma \vdash  A\equiv  B\space \text {type}
}{
    \Gamma \vdash  B\space \text {type}
}

\frac {
    \Gamma \vdash  a\equiv  b:A
}{
    \Gamma \vdash  a:A
}
\quad  
\frac {
    \Gamma \vdash  a\equiv  b:A
}{
    \Gamma \vdash  b:A
}

\frac {
    \Gamma \vdash  a:A
}{
    \Gamma \vdash  a\equiv  a:A
}
\quad 
\frac {
    \Gamma \vdash  a\equiv  b:A
}{
    \Gamma \vdash  b\equiv  a:A
}
\quad 
\frac {
    \Gamma \vdash  a\equiv  b:A
    \quad 
    \Gamma \vdash  b\equiv  c:A
}{
    \Gamma \vdash  a\equiv  c:A
}

\frac {
    \Gamma \vdash  A\space \text {type}
}{
    \Gamma \vdash  A\equiv  A\space \text {type}
}
\quad 
\frac {
    \Gamma \vdash  A\equiv  B\space \text {type}
}{
    \Gamma \vdash  B\equiv  A\space \text {type}
}
\quad 
\frac {
    \Gamma \vdash  A\equiv  B\space \text {type}
    \quad 
    \Gamma \vdash  B\equiv  C\space \text {type}
}{
    \Gamma \vdash  A\equiv  C\space \text {type}
}

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
This rule postulates that we can
convert the type of a variable to a judgmentally equal type.

    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  A\equiv  A'\space \text {type}
        \quad 
        \Gamma ,x:A,\Delta \vdash  B(x)\space \text {type}
    }{
        \Gamma ,x:A',\Delta \vdash  B(x)\space \text {type}
    }

Similarly, we can convert judgmental equality of types and terms.
We state all of them at once using a generic judgment thesis \mathcal {J}.

    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  A\equiv  A'\space \text {type}
        \quad 
        \Gamma ,x:A,\Delta \vdash  \mathcal {J}
    }{
        \Gamma ,x:A',\Delta \vdash  \mathcal {J}
    }VC

The substitution rule postulates that we can substitute a term for a variable.

    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  a:A
        \quad 
        \Gamma ,x:A,\Delta \vdash  \mathcal {J}
    }{
        \Gamma ,\Delta [x:=a]\vdash  \mathcal {J}[x:=a]
    }S

The notation \Gamma ,\Delta [x:=a] means that we substitute a for x in \Delta .

With the substitution rule, we need two more congruence rules to
convert judgmental equality of terms and types.

    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  a\equiv  a':A
        \quad 
        \Gamma ,x:A,\Delta \vdash  B \space \text {type}
    }{
        \Gamma ,\Delta [x:=a]\vdash  B[x:=a]\equiv  B[x:=a'] \space \text {type}
    }



    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  A\equiv  A'\space \text {type}
        \quad 
        \Gamma ,x:A,\Delta \vdash  b:A
    }{
        \Gamma ,\Delta [x:=a]\vdash  b[x:=a]\equiv  b[x:=a']:A'[x:=a] \space \text {type}
    }

Weakening rule asserts that we can add a variable to the context.

    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  A\space \text {type}
        \quad  
        \Gamma ,\Delta \vdash  \mathcal {J}
    }{
        \Gamma ,x:A,\Delta \vdash  \mathcal {J}
    }W

The rule for the generic element asserts that 
any hypothetical element x:A in context \Gamma ,x:A
is also an element of A in context \Gamma ,x:A.

    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  A\space \text {type}
    }{
        \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  x:A
    }\delta 

This rule is also called the variable rule.

Formation Rule
    For any type family B over A in context \Gamma :

        \frac {
            \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  B(x)\space \text {type}
        }{
            \Gamma \vdash  \Pi _{(x:A)}B(x)\space \text {type}
        }\Pi 

    We also require that the operation of forming dependent function types
    respects judgmental equality.

        \frac {
            \Gamma \vdash  A\equiv  A'\space \text {type}
            \quad 
            \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  B(x)\equiv  B'(x)\space \text {type}
        }{
            \Gamma \vdash  \Pi _{(x:A)}B(x)\equiv  \Pi _{(x:A')}B'(x)\space \text {type}
        }\Pi \text {-eq}

Introduction Rule (\lambda -abstraction)
    In order to construct a dependent function we have to
    construct a term f(x):B(x) indexed by x:A in context \Gamma :

        \frac {
            \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  b(x):B(x)
        }{
            \Gamma \vdash  \lambda  x.b(x):\Pi _{(x:A)}B(x)
        }\lambda 

    And the congruence rule:

        \frac {
            \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  b(x)\equiv  b'(x):B(x)
        }{
            \Gamma \vdash  \lambda  x.b(x)\equiv  \lambda  x.b'(x):\Pi _{(x:A)}B(x)
        }\lambda \text {-eq}

Elimination Rule (Evaluation Rule)
    In order to use dependent function we need to provide an argument of the domain type.

        \frac {
            \Gamma \vdash  f:\Pi _{(x:A)}B(x)
        }{
            \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  f(x):B(x)
        }ev

    Again we require the judgmental equality to be respected:

        \frac {
            \Gamma \vdash  f\equiv  f':\Pi _{(x:A)}B(x)
        }{
            \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  f(x)\equiv  f'(x):B(x)
        }ev\text {-eq}

Computation Rule (\beta -reduction)

        \frac {
            \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  b(x):B(x)
        }{
            \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  (\lambda  y.b(y))(x)\equiv  b(x):B(x)
        }\beta 

    We postulate that all elements of a dependent function type are dependent functions.

        \frac {
            \Gamma \vdash  f:\Pi _{(x:A)}B(x)
        }{
            \Gamma \vdash  f\equiv  \lambda  x.f(x):\Pi _{(x:A)}B(x)
        }\eta

A special case of \Pi -type is the ordinary function type.
Using weakening rule we can obtain thee type A\to  B of ordinary function from A to B

    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  A\space \text {type}
        \quad 
        \Gamma \vdash  B\space \text {type}
    }{\dfrac {
        \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  B\space \text {type}
    }{
        \Gamma \vdash  \Pi _{(x:A)}B\space \text {type}
    }\Pi }W

A term f: \Pi _{(x:A)}B is an ordinary function. The type A \to  B is defined:

    A\to  B := \Pi _{(x:A)}B

The type A is called domain of f,
and type B is called codomain of f.
The notation := here means to make a definition.

In type theory, natural number is defined using peano encoding.
The type \mathbb {N} is formed by the formation rule:

    \frac {}{\vdash \mathbb {N}\space \text {type}}\mathbb {N}\text {-form}

Peano's first axiom postulates the existence of a natural number 0.
The introduction rule for \mathbb {N} has a 0 constructor and a successor function.

    \frac {}{\vdash 0:\mathbb {N}}\mathbb {N}\text {-intro-0}
    \quad 
    \frac {}{\vdash \text {succ}:\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}} \mathbb {N}\text {-intro-succ}

The elimination rule is actually the type theoretical induction principle of \mathbb {N}:
In order to show that \forall  n:\mathbb {N}.P(n) holds, it suffices to show that P(0) holds and that \forall  n:\mathbb {N}.P(n)\to  P(\text {succ}(n)) holds.
The type theoretical induction principle is therefore formulated using a type family P over \mathbb {N}:

    \frac {
        \begin {align*}
            \Gamma &,\,n:\mathbb {N}\vdash  P(n)\space \text {type}
            \\
            \Gamma &\vdash  p_0:P(0)
            \\
            \Gamma &\vdash  p_S:\Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n)\to  P(\text {succ}(n))
        \end {align*}
    }{
        \Gamma \vdash \text {ind}_\mathbb {N}(p_0,p_S):\Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n)
    }(\mathbb {N} \text {-ind})

The induction principle tells us what we need to do in order to construct a dependent function \text {ind}_\mathbb {N} of type \Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n).
We might alternatively write the induction principle as:

    \frac {
        \Gamma ,n:\mathbb {N}\vdash  P(n)\space \text {type}
    }{
        \Gamma \vdash \text {ind}_\mathbb {N}:\left (P(0)\to \left (\Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n)\to  P(\text {succ}(n))\right )\to \Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n)\right )
    }(\mathbb {N} \text {-ind})

The computation rule asserts that the dependent function \text {ind}_\mathbb {N} behaves as expected:

    \frac {
        \begin {align*}
            \Gamma &,n:\mathbb {N}\vdash  P(n)\space \text {type}
            \\
            \Gamma &\vdash  p_0:P(0)
            \\
            \Gamma &\vdash  p_S:\Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n)\to  P(\text {succ}(n))
        \end {align*}
    }{
        \text {ind}_\mathbb {N}(p_0,p_S,m)\equiv 
        \begin {cases}
            p_0&\text {if }m=0
            \\
            p_S(n,\text {ind}_\mathbb {N}(p_0,p_S,n))&\text {if }m=\text {succ}(n)
        \end {cases}
    }(\mathbb {N} \text {-comp})

fr:tree.fr:mainmatter:



    We construct the addition by performing induction over the second variable n.
    That is, to construct an element

        m:\mathbb {N} \vdash  \text {add}_\mathbb {N}(m):\mathbb {N} \to  \mathbb {N}

    The context \Gamma  \equiv  m:\mathbb {N} is fixed.

Therefore we need to construct:

    \begin {align*}
        \Gamma &\vdash  \text {add-zero}_\mathbb {N}(m):\mathbb {N}
        \\
        \Gamma &\vdash  \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m):\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}                
    \end {align*}   

The \text {add-zero}_\mathbb {N} is defined to be identity function trivially. To see how \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N} is defined, we need to perform induction:

    \begin {align*}
        \text {add}_\mathbb {N}(m, \text {succ}(n))&\equiv 
        \text {ind}_\mathbb {N}(\text {add-zero}_\mathbb {N}(m), \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m), \text {succ}(n))
        \\
        &\equiv  \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m,n, \text {add}_\mathbb {N}(m,n))
        \\
        &\equiv  \text {succ}(\text {add}_\mathbb {N}(m,n))
    \end {align*}

Hence \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N} is defined as:

    \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m,n,x) \equiv  \text {succ}(x)

A formal construction of \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N} is as follows:

    \dfrac {
        \dfrac {
            \dfrac {
                \dfrac {}{\vdash \mathbb {N}\space \text {type}}
                \quad 
                \dfrac {
                    \dfrac {}{\vdash  \mathbb {N}\space \text {type}}
                    \quad 
                    \dfrac {}{\vdash  \text {succ}:\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}}
                }{
                    n:\mathbb {N}\vdash \text {succ}:\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}
                }
            }{
                m:\mathbb {N}, n:\mathbb {N}\vdash  \text {succ}:\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}
            } 
        }{
            m:\mathbb {N}\vdash \lambda  n.\text {succ} : \mathbb {N}\to (\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N})
        }
    }{
        m:\mathbb {N}\vdash  \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m):\equiv \lambda  m.\text {succ} : \mathbb {N}\to (\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N})
    }\text {Block-1}

Finally we combine the derivation together:

    \dfrac {
        \dfrac {
            \dfrac {
                \dfrac {
                    \vdash  \mathbb {N}\space \text {type}
                }{
                    m:\mathbb {N}\vdash  m:\mathbb {N}
                }
            }{
                m:\mathbb {N}\vdash \text {add-zero}_\mathbb {N}(m):\equiv  m:\mathbb {N}
            }
            \quad  
            \dfrac {\text {Block-1}}{
                m:\mathbb {N}\vdash \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m):\mathbb {N}\to (\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N})
            }
        }{
            m:\mathbb {N}\vdash \text {ind}_\mathbb {N} (\text {add-zero}_\mathbb {N}(m), \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m)):\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}
        }
    }{
        m:\mathbb {N}\vdash \text {add}_\mathbb {N}(m):\equiv \text {ind}_\mathbb {N} (\text {add-zero}_\mathbb {N}(m), \text {add-succ}_\mathbb {N}(m)):\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}
    }

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Recall the definition of addition function \text {add}: \mathbb {N}\to (\mathbb {N}\to \mathbb {N}) satisfying the specification:

    \begin {align*}
        m + 0 & :\equiv  m
        \\ 
        m + \text {succ}(n) & :\equiv  \text {succ}(m + n)
    \end {align*}

Such definition is enough to characterize the addition function.
Because it postulates te behavior of \text {add}_\mathbb {N} at the constructor of \mathbb {N}

More generally, we can define a dependent function f:\Pi  n:\mathbb {N}.P(n) by induction on n using

    \begin {align*}
        p_0 & : P(0)
        \\
        p_S & : \Pi _{(n:\mathbb {N})}P(n)\to  P(\text {succ}(n))
    \end {align*}

Just present the definition by writing

    \begin {align*}
        f(0) & :\equiv  p_0
        \\
        f(\text {succ}(n)) & :\equiv  p_S(n,f(n))
    \end {align*}

f is said to be defined by pattern matching on the variable n.

    Let A and B be types. The coproduct (disjoint sum) A+B is a typed defined by the following constructors:

\text {inl}:A\to  A+B

\text {inr}:B\to  A+B
    For any type family indexed by x:A+B, satisfies the following induction principle:

        \text {ind}_+: (\Pi _{(x:A)}P(\text {inl}(x)))\to (\Pi _{(y:B)}P(\text {inr}(y)))\to \Pi _{(z:A+B)}P(z)

    (Note that sometimes we denoted \text {ind}_+(f,g) as [f,g]) And the computation rule:

        \begin {align*}
            \text {ind}_+(f,g,\text {inl}(x))&\equiv  f(x)\\
            \text {ind}_+(f,g,\text {inr}(y))&\equiv  g(y)
        \end {align*}

    where f and g are defined:

        \begin {align*}
            f&:\Pi _{(x:A)}P(\text {inl}(x))\\
            g&:\Pi _{(y:B)}P(\text {inr}(y))
        \end {align*}

    This can be presented by pattern matching to define a function h:\Pi  _{(z:A+B)}.P(z):

        \begin {align*}
            h(\text {inl}(x))&:\equiv  f(x)\\
            h(\text {inr}(y))&:\equiv  g(y)
        \end {align*}

The elimination rule is formed with two projections.

    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  p:(x:A)\times  B(x)
    }{
        \begin {align*}
            \Gamma &\vdash  \text {pr}_A(p):A\\
            \Gamma &\vdash  \text {pr}_B(p):B(\text {pr}_A(p))
        \end {align*}
    }

Consider a type A and let a:A be an element.
The identity type of A at a is an inductive family of types a=_Ax 
indexed by x:A.

    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  a:A
    }{
        \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  a=_Ax\space \text {type}
    }

The only constructor is the refl:

    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  a:A
    }{
        \Gamma \vdash  \text {refl}_a:a=_Ax
    }

fr:tree.fr:mainmatter:
The induction principle of the identity type states that
for any type family P(x,p) indexed by x:A and p:a=_Ax,

    \text {ind-eq}_{a}:P(a, \text {refl}_a)\to  (x:A)\to  (p:a=_Ax)\to  P(x,p)

satisfies \text {ind-eq}_{a}(u,a,\text {refl}_a)\equiv  u where u:P(a,\text {refl}_a).
Formally we can write:

    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  a:A,\quad  \Gamma ,x:A,p:a=_Ax\vdash  P(x,p)\space \text {type}
    }{
        \Gamma \vdash  \text {ind-eq}_{a}(a,\text {refl}_a): P(a,\text {refl}_a) \to  (x:A)\to  (p:a=_Ax)\to  P(x,p)
    }\text {eq-elim}


    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  a:A,\quad  \Gamma ,x:A,p:a=_Ax\vdash  P(x,p)\space \text {type}
    }{
        \Gamma ,u:P(a,\text {refl}_a)\vdash  \text {ind-eq}_{a}(u,a,\text {refl}_a)\equiv  u:P(a,\text {refl}_a)
    }\text {eq-comp}

We can form an identity type with variables of A.

    \Gamma ,x:A,y:A\vdash  x=_Ay\space \text {type}

with the following introduction rule:

    \frac {
        \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  x:A
    }{
        \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  \text {refl}_x:x=_Ax
    }

and similarly the elimination rule and computation rule.

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Let A be a type.
We defined the unit law operations for x=_Ay:

    \begin {align*}
        \text {left-unit} & : (x=_Ay)\to  (\text {refl}_x\cdot  x = x) \\
        \text {right-unit} & : (x=_Ay)\to  (x\cdot \text {refl}_y = x)
    \end {align*}

fr:tree.fr:mainmatter:
By elimination it suffices to construct:

    \begin {align*}
        \text {left-unit}(\text {refl}_x) &: \text {refl}_x \cdot  \text {refl}_x = \text {refl}_x \\
        \text {right-unit}(\text {refl}_x) &: \text {refl}_x \cdot  \text {refl}_x = \text {refl}_x
    \end {align*}

In both cases we need only to construct \text {refl}_{\text {refl}_x}.

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Let f:A\to  B be a map. We define the action on paths of f as an operation:

    \text {ap}_f : \prod _{x,y:A} (x=y) \to  (f(x)=f(y))

This can be defined by induction principle of identity types:

    \text {ap}_f(\text {refl}_x) \equiv  \text {refl}_{f(x)}

And there are operations:

    \text {ap-id}_A:       \prod _{(x,y:A)}\prod _{(p:x=y)} p = \text {ap}_{\text {id}_A}(p)

Taking

    \text {ap-id}_A(\text {refl}_x) \equiv  \text {refl}_{\text {refl}_x}

Finally we can construct

    \text {ap-comp}(f, g): \prod _{(x,y:A)}\prod _{(p:x=y)} \text {ap}_{f\circ  g}(p) = \text {ap}_f(\text {ap}_g(p))

by taking

    \text {ap-comp}(f,g,\text {refl}_x) \equiv  \text {refl}_{\text {refl}_{g(f(x))}}

There are identifications:


        Notice that we have \text {ap-id}_A(\text {refl}_x) \equiv  \text {refl}_{\text {refl}_x} so

            \begin {align*}
                \text {ap-refl}(f,x) &:\text {ap}_f(\text {refl}_x) = \text {refl}_{f(x)} \\
                \text {ap-refl}(f,x) &\equiv  \text {refl}_{\text {refl}_{f(x)}}
            \end {align*}



        The \text {ap-inv} can be constructed by identification elimination on p.

            \begin {align*}
                &\text {ap-inv}(f,p):\text {ap}_f(p^{-1}) = \text {ap}_f(p)^{-1}\\
                &\text {ap-inv}(f,\text {refl}_x)\equiv  \text {refl}_{\text {ap}_f(\text {refl}_x)}
            \end {align*}



        Similarly, the \text {ap-concat} can be constructed by identification elimination on p.

            \begin {align*}
                &\text {ap-concat}(f,p,q):\text {ap}_f(p\cdot  q) = \text {ap}_f(p)\cdot  \text {ap}_f(q) \\
                &\text {ap-concat}(f,\text {refl}_x,q)\equiv  \text {refl}_{\text {ap}_f(q)}
            \end {align*}

We assume that there're enough universe, i.e.,
for every finite list of types in context 

    \Gamma _1\vdash  A_1\space \space \text {type},\cdots ,\Gamma _n\vdash  A_n\space \space \text {type}

there is a universe \mathcal {U} that contains each A_i in the sense that \mathcal {U} comes equipped with

    \Gamma _i\vdash \check {A_i}:\mathcal {U}

for which the following judgement holds:

    \Gamma _i \vdash \mathcal {T}(\check {A_i})\equiv  A_i\space \space \text {type}

The successor universee \mathcal {U}^+ of a universe \mathcal {U} is defined using universe postulate
with the following contexts:

    \vdash \mathcal {U}\space \space \text {type}\quad  X:\mathcal {U}\vdash \mathcal {T}(X)\space \space \text {type}

It therefore contains the type \mathcal {U} as well as type in \mathcal {U}, in the following sense

    \begin {align*}
        &\vdash \check {\mathcal {U}}:\mathcal {U}^+\\ 
        &\vdash \mathcal {T}^+(\check {\mathcal {U}})\equiv \mathcal {U}\space \text {type}\\
        &X:\mathcal {U}\vdash \check {\mathcal {T}}(X):\mathcal {U}^+\\ 
        &X:\mathcal {U}\vdash \mathcal {T}^+(\check {\mathcal {T}}(X))\equiv \mathcal {T}(X)\space \text {type} 
    \end {align*}

 The observational equality of \mathbb {N} is a binary relation \text {Eq}_\mathbb {N}:\mathbb {N}\to (\mathbb {N}\to \mathcal {U}_0) satisfies

     \begin {align*}
         \text {Eq}_\mathbb {N}(0)(0) &\equiv  \textbf {1} \\
         \text {Eq}_\mathbb {N}(0)(\text {succ}(n)) &\equiv  \textbf {0} \\
         \text {Eq}_\mathbb {N}(\text {succ}(m))(0) &\equiv  \textbf {0} \\
         \text {Eq}_\mathbb {N}(\text {succ}(m))(\text {succ}(n)) &\equiv  \text {Eq}_\mathbb {N}(m)(n)
     \end {align*}




proof
     We define \text {Eq} by double induction on \mathbb {N}. By the first application of induction
     it suffices to provide

         \begin {align*}
             &E_0:\mathbb {N}\to \mathcal {U}_0 \\ 
             &E_S:\mathbb {N}\to ((\mathbb {N}\to \mathcal {U}_0)\to (\mathbb {N}\to \mathcal {U}_0))
         \end {align*}

     we define E_0 by induction:

         \begin {align*}
             &E_0(0) \equiv \textbf {1}\\ 
             &E_0(\text {succ}(n)) \equiv \textbf {0}
         \end {align*}

     and also define E_S by induction:

         \begin {align*}
             &E_S(n,X,0)\equiv \textbf {0}\\ 
             &E_S(n,X,\text {succ}(m))\equiv  X(m)
         \end {align*}

     Therefore we have by the computation rule for the first induction that 
     the following judgemental equality holds.

         \begin {align*}
             \text {Eq}(0,m) &\equiv  E_0(m) \\
             \text {Eq}(\text {succ}(n),m) &\equiv  E_S(n,\text {Eq}(n),m)
         \end {align*}

The wavelength of a wave function \Psi is related to the momentum of the particle. This result from the de Broglie formula.
p = \frac {h}{\lambda } = \frac {2\pi \hbar }{\lambda }
Now we can give a qualitative argument for the uncertainty principle.

The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle states that it is impossible to measure simultaneously the position and the momentum of a particle with arbitrary precision. The product of the uncertainties in position and momentum is bounded by:
\Delta x \Delta p \geq \frac {\hbar }{2}
where \Delta x and \Delta p are the uncertainties in position and momentum, respectively. The constant \hbar is the reduced Planck constant.

The light-cone coordinates can be defined as two independent linear combinations of the time and a chosen spatial coordinate (conventionally x^1):
\begin {align*} x^+ \equiv \frac {1}{\sqrt {2}} (X^0 + X^1) \\ x^- \equiv \frac {1}{\sqrt {2}} (X^0 - X^1) \end {align*}
while other spatial coordinates remain unchanged. Thus the complete set of light-cone coordinates is (x^+,x^-,x^2,x^3).

            Now let's try to construct some graphs using the Relation type and the Num instance:

  ghci> 1 * (2 + 3) :: Relation Int
R {domain = fromList [1,2,3], relation = fromList [(1,2),(1,3)]}
ghci> 1 * 2 * 3 :: Relation Int
R {domain = fromList [1,2,3], relation = fromList [(1,2),(1,3),(2,3)]}
